\f1\fs22 St Hedges Cathedral singularly proved that the thinking processes of the Airborne didn\'92t always draw the straightest line between the itch of greed and the quick return of profit. That is, if you ignore the more egregious attempts of the Country Party to pork-barrel taxes away from the cities and straight into the hands of mining magnates.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cathedral and its environs was undoubtably the costliest capital expenditure project in the history of the Great Meritocracy of Ulyssia. Ostentatious, stately, awe-inspiring, and a triumph of engineering\'97these were just some of superlatives used to describe the cathedral\'92s bathrooms.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cathedral itself existed on another plane of grandeur altogether. So too the grounds, which nestled in a nine-sided shallow bowl-shaped platform a hundred metres above the Layer. It covered more than 10 hectares in area, more than 1 billion square centimetres, because the venture capitalists who sat on the board of St Hedges Permanent Holdings and Stranglings LLC demanded at least one measurement of its grandiosity should exceed 1 billion somethings. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At each of the nine corners of the platform, a statue of one of the Nine Major Saints of Latter Day Capitalism looked out, valiant and imposing, over the sun-drenched cerulean skies. They saw all who came and went, each guarding a hover-pads stuck to the platform like a huge, grey, stale lollipop.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the centre of the platform, St Hedges presided over spectacular sweeping lawns and beds of flowers framed by topiaries of geometrically trimmed hedges and imposing copses of trees. The flower beds were of all the most extraordinary colours of the rainbow except red, which was, of course, an unwelcome colour, representing bad news in politics, or on any financial report. Following the same construct of good and evil, the lawns were all black, representing perennial profit, and the death  of socialism.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Black grass, you say? True! Horticulturalists were hired to cultivate a new breed of grass, the colour of which was aptly called Accountancy Black.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Wide, sweeping stone paths curved through the grounds and converged on the cathedral, which both inspired and conquered the fiscal souls of all who approached. Incorporating advanced metallurgy for the structure, and brutalist masonry for the facades of limestone and bluestone (the local sandstone having been discarded for its tendency to age from virtuous yellow gold to shitty brown), it was its clever use of glass ceilings which invariably struck a first-time visitor dumb, reminding them of the limitlessness of the skies, both literally and in the metaphorical opportunistic sense.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everything about the cathedral and its environs was designed to impress. No expense in the construction and detailing of the cathedral was spared because every Airborne knew, in the warm cockles of their wallets and purses, an impressed person was a distracted person, and a distracted person was easier to rob.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The floors of the cathedral nave were paved in the most flawless marble, the pews hewn from the most endangered of hardwoods, and the pew cushions for easing the aches of the richest arses were reportedly made of the luxurious hides of mink (or perhaps seal\'97both kind of farmed animal had been genetically engineered to grow plump, square and cushion-shaped). Intricate stained-glass windows depicted minor prophets of Latter Day Capitalism doling out enshittified social network subscriptions to the huddled masses. Adorning the walls were graphs and tables inlaid with precious jewels and metals that glinted, scintillated and coruscated as they caught light from the windows and the bling of congregations. If the light was right, the trend lines and numbers of key holy indicators such as customer acquisition cost, customer lifetime value, recurring revenue, churn rate, and net promoter score seemed to leap off the walls and come alive.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The actual seating capacity of the nave is Commercial in Confidence, but has been estimated in various financial markets to be somewhere between 3000 and 23 billion. Curiously, no one has ever bothered to count the seats, but the St Hedges Cathedral Preservation Society (incorporated on Kangaroo Island) consistently boasts, in their annual reports, a seating capacity growth rate of 23% year-on-year. No one actually believes this, but the mere publication of the numbers seems sufficient to ward off disgruntled investors and hostile take-overs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Whether the cathedral seated 3000 or 23 billion, the congregation attending the holy joint venture of Alexander Koalemos and Sarah Jacobson were too few to suffocate all the plump, square platypus cushions (or were they otter? ), even as their arse cheeks were privilege-flabby. Initially, Alexanders\'92s side of the aisle made a better show of it, but that was mainly due to the number of production crews working every angle of the ceremony, including an in-depth piece about the Accountancy Black lawns, and whether they really were just painted that colour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s side of the nave was barely one third full. That is, until the Elvi showed up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Curiously, many of the Elvi were carrying in their arms, bundles of light fabric and tangles of ropes that cold have been parachutes. Despite apparently parachute-crashing the exclusive ceremony, all their hair was perfect.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The younger Airborne attendees, being fans of the overnight success of Elvi music, were enraptured by the mass appearance of their cultural heroes. Cries of, \'91We love you, Elvi\'92, \'91I want to have your babies\'92, and \'91Sing 
\f2\i Evolve Me Tender
\f1\i0 \'92 disrupted the decorum of the solemn mood. Older, more conservative Airborne would have nothing of such an offensive intrusion by lazy, low-class minimum-wage-abusers, and with dismissive waves of angry protest, filed out of the pews towards the exits.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander panicked; the status of his moment in the sun was draining, so he did what he did best: he flashed a gormless expression of confusion at his senior associate producer, who quickly understood the non-verbal plea: 
\f2\i For tax\'92s sake, fix this!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The associate producer ordered all cameras on site be aimed like weapons at the fleeing Airborne. Faced with the probability of a blitz of unflattering coverage at the hands of Alexander\'92s publicity machine, most considered what a shame it would be to miss the reception afterwards, and returned to their seats.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi continued to file in until all remaining seat cushions were flattened, and the event became standing room only, which is to say the marketing department could plausibly claim the crowd numbered more than 23 billion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Among the invited dignitaries in the second row, Piotr Dunhilton clenched his jaw with determination as the tides of Airborne and Elvi ebbed and flowed. With narrow eyes he watched Sarah, whose countervailing fear and exhilaration made theatre of her face. She brushed a tear from her eye and smiled. Dunhilton thought he saw relief in the way she exhaled and tension melted from her shoulders. In the soft curl of her gentle smile and the flushing of her cheeks, he thought he saw affection. Sarah Jacobson had affection for these clones. Dunhilton saw her affection and didn\'92t like it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With apoplexy, the Very Arch Bishop of Business of Ulyssia, Ignatius d\'92Molesto, watched the mob sweating in his cathedral, and lamented the inadequacy of the security he had bought for the event. With resignation, he huffed and sighed  from his very comfortable, well-cushioned throne.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis entered the nave to cheers from his flock, strode up the aisle and stood before the Very Arch Bishop. They regarded each other and, when the High Elvis realised the Bishop would not vacate the throne for him, he turned and stood proudly at the right side.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At precisely that moment, sunlight poured through the stained glass windows and fell just so, reflecting off the precious jewels and metals in all the graphs and tables etched into the walls of the nave. By an incalculable accident of geometry, much of the reflected light struck pinpoints in the sunglasses and jewellery of the High Elvis, and from there shone on the congregation with the intensity of a thousand mirror balls in a thousand night clubs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi gazed upon the spectacle with awe and amazement, and knew beyond a certainty it was a sign. (Just what kind of sign it was, they would have to ask the Great Teacher later, as he had declined to attend the ceremony.) As one, they took to their feet, enfolded their arms in the manner of cradling a newborn baby, and chanted, \'91Holy Momma! Holy Momma!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton became all the more alarmed as the chanting rang out and the very pregnant Sarah smiled with embarrassment. Dunhilton realised she was accepting the title as hers. 
\f2\i Sarah Jacobson is the Holy Momma!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The earth turned and rolled along its orbit, the sunbeams tilted, and the High Elvis was no longer cast as a being of light. He stepped forward and waved the Elvi chanting down. When reverent silence had been restored, the Very Arch Bishop rose and pompously strode to the pulpit to begin the ceremony, taking the utmost care that his robes swished perfectly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis nicked his seat.
